--TEST--
`Core\Budget::memoryLimit` is the ceiling this request is measured against and not a reading of it — it does not move when the request allocates, and an uncapped request answers `0` rather than a ceiling of its own — `rule:observability/memory-is-three-numbers-on-core-budget`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The member exists so that a peak has a scale, so what it must *not* be is a
// third figure derived from the same counter. This case allocates between two
// readings of it: a ceiling is a property of the request and answers the same
// number on both sides, where a reading would move.
uint $limit = Core\Budget::memoryLimit();

string $spike = Core\Str::repeat("y", 2097152);
if (Core\Budget::memoryLimit() == $limit) {
    echo "limit: unmoved by an allocation\n";
} else {
    echo "limit: moved with the request's own memory\n";
}
$spike = "";

// Both arms print the same line on purpose. Whether this runner arms a
// `[limits] memory` at all is not this case's question, and a case that pinned
// one answer would pin the runner's configuration instead of the member: what
// is asserted is that the two readings are comparable in the one expression
// the rule is written around, and that `0` means *no ceiling* rather than a
// ceiling of zero bytes that every request is already over.
if ($limit == 0) {
    echo "limit: uncapped, or a ceiling the peak is under\n";
} else {
    if (Core\Budget::memoryPeak() < $limit) {
        echo "limit: uncapped, or a ceiling the peak is under\n";
    } else {
        echo "limit: this request is already over its own ceiling\n";
    }
}
--EXPECT--
limit: unmoved by an allocation
limit: uncapped, or a ceiling the peak is under
